WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.250
Look at this glass!

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.340
What do you see inside it?

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.420
Many of you would probably say
that it’s empty!

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.750
However it’s not.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.000
Then what’s inside it?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.380
There is AIR inside this
glass!

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.040
Yes, air is present everywhere around us.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.020
But tell me, is air heavy?

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.340
We
generally feel that air is light in weight.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.220
How true is this?

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.000
Let us do an experiment
to see how heavy the air is!

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.110
Take a bottle filled with water.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.770
Now take a piece of cardboard and place it
on the

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.730
open end of the bottle!

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.500
Hold the cardboard with your hand and invert
the bottle

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.140
upside down!

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.810
Now take off your hand from the cardboard.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.120
What did you see?

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.829
The
cardboard sticks to the surface.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:54.400
This is because the air’s pressure is pushing
the

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.820
piece of cardboard in the upward direction!

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.190
Does this mean that air is heavier
than the water?

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:05.170
Well, the air is present everywhere, and hence
it is exerting

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.340
pressure from ALL THE DIRECTIONS.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:13.380
Clearly the air pressure is more than the
pressure of the water inside the bottle.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:18.470
And this allows the piece of the cardboard
to stay on the surface of the bottle!

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:23.409
With this activity we can understand that
AIR EXERTS PRESSURE.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:27.500
You may note
that air pressure acts in ALL DIRECTIONS!

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.570
Do you know why?

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.570
It’s because air is a
fluid.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:35.920
The molecules of air are very loosely held
and hence they can easily move

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.560
in all the directions.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.090
We all know that the earth is surrounded by
an ENVELOPE OF AIR.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.440
Do you know
what is it called?

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.610
It is called as ATMOSPHERE.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:53.030
And this atmosphere is divided into
different layers as shown in this image.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.010
What can we say about the air in the atmosphere?

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.360
The air in the atmosphere exerts pressure
on the earth or we can say on the

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.780
earth’s objects.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.630
What’s this pressure called?

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:10.369
The pressure exerted by air is called
as AIR PRESSURE or ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE.

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:14.850
Now the question is whether air pressure is
uniform throughout these layers of

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.519
atmosphere…

00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:17.910
Think about it.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.260
Where would the maximum quantity of air be?

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.120
There is maximum air above the troposphere.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.239
There’s this much air above the
troposphere.

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:30.760
Then this much air above the stratosphere.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.359
And this much above the
Mesosphere and so on.

00:02:34.359 --> 00:02:37.890
So as we move upwards, the quantum of air
decreases.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:43.950
Now logically, can you tell me which layer
carries the maximum air pressure?

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.639
Yes
the bottom most layer carries the weight of

00:02:46.639 --> 00:02:50.410
the air present in all the layers above
it.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:55.030
And that’s the reason why the air pressure
is maximum at this layer…

00:02:55.030 --> 00:03:00.599
And what can you say about the air pressure
at the layers above this point?

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:03.480
The
air pressure decreases as we move higher.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.659
Does air move?

00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:08.040
If yes, what’s it called?

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.079
Moving air is called WIND.

00:03:11.079 --> 00:03:14.069
Have you ever noticed the SPEED of the wind?

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:16.370
What
happens to the AIR PRESSURE when the speed

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.680
of the wind is INCREASED?

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.109
We will
find out the answers to this question in our

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:24.089
next lesson.

